# Everything tracked here is text - the repository has no batch or PowerShell files - and
# .editorconfig asks for LF everywhere already. This is that same rule enforced at
# checkout, and it is what makes the pre-commit gate usable on Windows: with
# core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows default) a checkout writes CRLF, and
# `gofmt -l` - which `make check` fails on - then reports every Go file as unformatted.
# The gate cannot pass on such a machine at all, and none of it has anything to do with
# the code. `text=auto` keeps genuine binaries binary, since git sniffs those.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell entry points are written to be executed on Linux, and install.sh is downloaded
# and run as-is: a carriage return in its hashbang or inside a heredoc breaks it, which is
# exactly what happens to a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true. Keep them LF, and
# spell out `text` so the content sniffing above cannot decide otherwise.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The build tag list is read by install.sh and by the release workflow; a trailing CR
# would land inside the -tags value and the build would fail with an unknown tag.
release/TAGS text eol=lf
